                    briefing_text_r1:       'The Q1 tracker: satisfaction <strong>78% → 69%</strong>, brand equity 64 → 58, and a service-quality gap of <strong>1.8 points</strong>, the widest recorded, measured across reliability, responsiveness, assurance, empathy and tangibles. The internal diagnostic: branch staff wait <strong>4.2 days</strong> for an IT ticket and <strong>6 days</strong> for a credit approval against a one-day standard; front-line engagement is 61%. Branches average 34 minutes against an advertised 15. The bank is losing ~190,000 customers a year, and each point of annual attrition is worth ~₱410M. Put your diagnosis in writing for the board.',
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Build internal marketing orientation`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       'Set the internal terms. You can leave IT and credit without a service standard, issue a one-day standard as a target, or set a two-day standard funded with capacity, recognition and shared measurement. Decide how much authority a branch employee holds to settle a complaint on the spot, how you move the internal departments, and how much of the <strong>₱600M</strong> (up to ₱300M) you commit to this work now.',
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set the standard and allocate ₱600M`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       'Divide the money and re-set the promise. Advertising takes ₱60M, ₱180M or ₱350M; customer-facing service (queues, digital self-service, branch layout, training) takes ₱80M, ₱180M or ₱300M; you can add a top-up to the internal work. The three slices plus your Round 2 commitment must fit inside <strong>₱600M</strong>. Branches averaged 34 minutes in Q1; operations judged 22 minutes reachable by Q3 with the investments available. The advertised standard is yours to set.',
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the plan and absorb a shock`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       'Present to the board and absorb a shock: a viral complaint about a broken 15-minute promise, a neobank launching zero-fee accounts behind a ₱300M campaign, or a CFO demand for a 20% mid-year cut. Choose what your defence leads with, how you respond, the one indicator you will watch monthly, and how far you will hold the plan through a quarter in which the numbers may not yet move.',

