                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_internal:      `<strong>That’s the diagnosis Kang’s chain points to.</strong> The gap originates upstream: branch staff wait 4.2 days for IT and 6 for credit against a 1-day standard. External service quality is produced by internal service quality — fix the back office and the front line can finally keep the promise.`,
                    coach_r1_frontline:     `<strong>You’ve framed it as a front-line skill problem.</strong> But your own diagnostic shows tellers waiting 4.2 days for IT and 6 for credit. You’re about to train people to smile while the back office hands them broken tools. Kang’s research says the gap is upstream — so where should the first peso go?`,
                    coach_r1_advertising:   '<strong>A louder campaign is the trap, not the cause.</strong> You are already spending ₱220M a year promising 15-minute service against a 34-minute reality. Advertising a promise you can’t keep more loudly accelerates the decline; it doesn’t reverse it.',
                    coach_r1_customers:     `<strong>Blaming the customers ends the diagnosis before it starts.</strong> Expectations didn’t change overnight; your internal service did. A SERVQUAL gap is expectation <em>minus perception</em> — and the perception fell because the operation slipped, not because customers got greedy.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Reliability and responsiveness are exactly where the 34-vs-15-minute failure and the IT/credit turnaround live — and you’ve traced them to internal service, not the counter. That’s the symptom-vs-cause distinction the board needs before it funds anything.`,
                    coach_r1_tangibles:     `<strong>Tangibles aren’t the worst dimension here.</strong> A branch refresh looks like progress, but the evidence — 34-minute service against a 15-minute promise, multi-day internal turnaround — sits squarely in reliability and responsiveness. New signage won’t close a 1.8-point gap.`,
                    coach_r1_empathy:       `<strong>Empathy is the comforting answer, not the evidenced one.</strong> “Smile more” trains the symptom. The data points to reliability and responsiveness — promises kept on time — which the front line can’t deliver until the internal turnaround is fixed.`,
                    coach_r1_symptomboth:   `<strong>If low satisfaction is both symptom and cause, you’ll spend on the wrong thing.</strong> Treating the score directly is treating a fever without finding the infection. Kang’s chain says the cause is upstream in internal service — name that, or every peso chases the symptom.`,
                    coach_r1_symptomads:    `<strong>You’ve made weak advertising the cause.</strong> But brand equity is falling <em>because</em> the promise outruns the experience — more advertising widens that gap. The cause is internal service quality; the campaign is downstream of it.`,
                    coach_r1_inaction:      `<strong>You barely sized the cost of inaction.</strong> The board funds urgency it can see: ~190,000 customers a year at ₱410M per attrition point. Quantify the bleed, or you’ll be asking for ₱600M against a problem the board still thinks is cosmetic.`,
                    coach_r2_orientation:   `<strong>That is internal marketing orientation.</strong> A resourced, realistic SLA, an empowered front line, and a culture that treats colleagues as internal customers — recognition and shared scorecards, not just a mandate. Engagement climbs now; the satisfaction lift lands next quarter as the chain works through.`,
                    coach_r2_mandate:       `<strong>You mandated a 1-day SLA with no capacity and no recognition.</strong> SLAs without internal marketing are just new targets to miss — in a quarter IT resents the front line more, not less. What makes a department <em>want</em> to serve its colleagues?`,
                    coach_r2_nosla:         `<strong>No internal SLA leaves the root cause untouched.</strong> If IT and credit stay cost centres with no service standard, the 4.2- and 6-day waits persist and the front line keeps handing customers an apology instead of a resolution.`,
                    coach_r2_noempower:     `<strong>With no front-line authority, every exception still routes upward.</strong> The service-recovery paradox only pays off when staff can resolve on the spot — route it upward and a recoverable complaint becomes a lost customer.`,
                    coach_r2_unlimited:     `<strong>Unlimited authority overnight is empowerment without a system.</strong> Front-line discretion needs limits and recovery playbooks, or you trade slow-but-controlled for fast-but-inconsistent. Empower with a cap, not a blank cheque.`,
                    coach_r2_orders:        `<strong>Issuing the SLA as a top-down order isn’t internal marketing.</strong> Compliance gets you grudging minimums; orientation gets you a department that wants the front line to win. Internal orientation is cultural and incentive-driven, not merely contractual.`,
                    coach_r2_ignoreculture: '<strong>Leaving culture alone treats this as a numbers problem.</strong> The numbers are produced by people who currently see the front line as someone else’s problem. Leave the culture alone and the standard decays the moment you stop watching.',
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>You’re advertising a promise the operation can’t keep.</strong> Promising service faster than your real branch time manufactures disappointment with every new customer the ad attracts. Which builds more equity — a promise kept at 22 minutes, or one broken at 15?`,
                    coach_r3_adfix:         `<strong>₱350M into the campaign, and the operation still runs behind the promise.</strong> Reach is excellent — and that’s the problem. Every new customer the ad attracts experiences the gap firsthand, so satisfaction drops <em>because</em> the ad worked. What did the campaign actually buy you?`,
                    coach_r3_evensplit:     `<strong>Three neat slices — tidy, and a tell.</strong> An even split signals you haven’t named the binding constraint. If internal service is the root cause, a one-third allocation under-resources the fix and over-resources the advertising that’s currently hurting you.`,
                    coach_r3_underinternal: `<strong>You under-funded the internal root fix.</strong> Advertising and a branch refresh dress the window, but the 4.2- and 6-day internal waits are still there. The gap won’t close until the back office can serve the front line — that’s where the constraint binds.`,
                    coach_r3_allocation:    `<strong>That allocation follows your diagnosis.</strong> Internal marketing funded first, a credible promise you can keep, a measured share-of-voice and real customer-facing investment. You’re spending behind the constraint, not against it — and the ROI will show it.`,
                    coach_r4_chain:         `<strong>That’s the defense a board trusts.</strong> Internal orientation → service quality → satisfaction → brand equity → ROI, with the numbers underneath each arrow. You’re not selling a campaign; you’re showing the mechanism — and that’s what holds the budget through the lag.`,
                    coach_r4_defenseads:    `<strong>Leading with reach invites the obvious question.</strong> “Impressions are up — so why is satisfaction still below target?” Awareness of a promise you can’t keep isn’t equity; it’s exposure. The board can see the satisfaction line.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Promising 75% next quarter denies the lag — and breaks your own credibility.</strong> The chain has lags: internal change moves engagement, then perception, a quarter apart. Promise an instant jump and you’ll report ‘no improvement’ at exactly the moment you should be holding the line.`,
                    coach_r4_viral_broken:  `<strong>The viral complaint landed on a promise you were still over-stating.</strong> You can’t recover gracefully from a gap you’re still advertising — the apology rings hollow next to the 15-minute billboard. A kept promise is what makes recovery believable.`,
                    coach_r4_neobank:       `<strong>Matching the blitz peso-for-peso abandons the strategy.</strong> You can’t out-shout a neobank, and trying drains the budget from the internal fix that is your only durable differentiation. Compete on the service you can deliver, not the share-of-voice you can’t.`,
                    coach_r4_cfocut:        `<strong>Taking the cut from internal marketing re-opens the root cause.</strong> The visible campaign survives; the back-office fix that actually moves satisfaction doesn’t. In a quarter the gap widens again — you protected the symptom and cut the cure.`,
                    coach_r4_indicator:     `<strong>The right leading indicator.</strong> Internal turnaround and front-line engagement move first; satisfaction follows a quarter later. Watch the upstream number monthly and you’ll see the recovery coming before the tracker prints it — and you can reassure the board through the lag.`,
                    coach_r4_awareness:     `<strong>Ad awareness is a lagging vanity number here.</strong> It tells you the campaign ran, not whether the service is recovering. The leading indicator is upstream — internal turnaround and engagement — because that’s what moves satisfaction next.`,
                    coach_r4_lagindicator:  `<strong>Watching the satisfaction score itself is watching the rear-view mirror.</strong> By the time the quarterly tracker moves, the cause moved a quarter earlier. Monitor the internal leading indicator so you can act before, not after.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Traced the gap upstream to internal service; named reliability & responsiveness.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Treated the symptom — misread the source of the gap.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Resourced SLA, capped empowerment, internal-customer culture.`,
                    sum_r2_unresourced:     `Mandated an SLA with no capacity or recognition.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Internal-marketing plan only partly built.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Internal-led allocation; a credible, kept promise.`,
                    sum_r3_adfix:           `Advertised the fix — amplified a promise the operation can’t keep.`,
                    sum_r3_overpromise:     `Kept an undeliverable promise — manufactured disappointment.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Allocation didn’t fully follow the diagnosis.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Defended the chain; absorbed the shock without abandoning the strategy.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Led with reach, matched the blitz, or cut the cure.`,

                    insight_orientation:    `Service brand restored: you ran the genuine internal-marketing pattern — diagnosed the cause upstream, resourced the internal SLA and treated the front line as internal customers, then allocated the ₱600M behind that constraint with a promise you could actually keep. Satisfaction cleared the 75% target, the service-quality gap closed, and brand equity recovered because the experience finally matched the promise.`,
                    insight_adfix:          'Advertised the fix: ₱350M went into a campaign behind a promise the branches could not keep, so the wider the reach, the more customers met a slower branch than the advertisement promised. Satisfaction fell because the campaign worked.',
                    insight_misdiagnosed:   `Treated the symptom: by locating the cause at the counter, in the customers, or in the advertising rather than upstream in internal service, the spend chased the satisfaction score instead of the failure that produced it — and the gap stayed open.`,
                    insight_mixed:          'No single move broke the run, but the diagnosis, the internal terms, the allocation and the promise did not add up to every board target.',
                    insight_overpromise:    'The brand-promise gap stayed open: you advertised {p} minutes while the branches ran {t}. Every customer the advertising reached was set up for a longer wait than promised, and the broken promise capped satisfaction below the target for the rest of the run.',
                    insight_floor:          `Satisfaction fell below the 65% floor: on this trajectory the board cuts the marketing budget and pivots to pure price competition — abandoning the service-led brand that was Maharlika’s only durable differentiation.`,
                    insight_sla:            'The internal standard was a target without a system: a one-day standard mandated with no added capacity or recognition, so IT and credit had one more number to miss and the improvement it queued was small.',
                    insight_gap_open:       `The service-quality gap never fully closed: the experience still trails the expectation, so even a recovering satisfaction score sits on fragile ground — the next disappointment re-opens it.`,
                    insight_brand_lag:      `Satisfaction recovered faster than brand equity: equity rebuilds slowly after the promise has been broken before, so the tracker will trail the satisfaction line for several quarters even on a sound plan.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You committed more than the ₱600M cap: the budget is fixed, and over-allocation isn’t a plan the board can fund — discipline on the cap is part of the credibility.`,

                    hint_floor:             'Satisfaction is below the 65% floor: on the board’s terms, the budget is cut and the bank competes on price.',
                    hint_overpromise:       'The advertised standard is faster than the branches now deliver.',
                    hint_adfix:             'On the board’s reading, the plan is now advertising-led.',
                    hint_misdiagnosed:      'Your diagnosis is on the record. Before you commit money, ask which number in the case is not itself a customer perception.',
                    hint_internal:          'Internal service work has received little of the budget so far.',
                    hint_strong:            'Satisfaction is past the target and the service-quality gap is at or below 1.0.',

